      en: "The Qinling Mountains run east-west across central China, dividing the country into northern and southern regions. Stretching about 1,500 km, the highest peak is Mount Taibai at 3,767 meters. This range is a critical climatic boundary: to the north lies a temperate zone with cold winters, to the south a humid subtropical climate. The mountains are a biodiversity hotspot, home to endangered species like the golden snub-nosed monkey and giant panda. The Qinling also serve as a watershed for both the Yellow River and Yangtze River basins.",
